# The order-two Vélu quotient of an elliptic curve is elliptic

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.isElliptic_veluQuotient2_of_isElliptic`
(`58581676-128b-54e4-af7c-3fd08fcd6b62`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `8dc32c81-17db-535c-848e-7af2eb323c94`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F$ be a field and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $F$ with coefficients
$a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4,a_6$ whose discriminant $\Delta$ is a unit, i.e. $W$ is elliptic. Let $x_0,y_0 \in
F$ satisfy the affine Weierstrass equation of $W$, so that $y_0^2 + a_1 x_0 y_0 + a_3 y_0 = x_0^3 +
a_2 x_0^2 + a_4 x_0 + a_6$, and suppose in addition that $W.veluGy(x_0,y_0) = -(2y_0 + a_1 x_0 +
a_3) = 0$, which says that the point $(x_0,y_0)$ is fixed by the Weierstrass involution, i.e. is a
point of order two. Write $g_x = W.veluGx(x_0,y_0) = 3x_0^2 + 2a_2 x_0 + a_4 - a_1 y_0$. The
conclusion is that the Weierstrass curve $W.veluQuotient2(x_0,y_0)$ with coefficients $a_1$, $a_2$,
$a_3$, $a_4 - 5 g_x$ and $a_6 - b_2 g_x - 7 x_0 g_x$ is again elliptic, i.e. its discriminant is a
unit in $F$.

This is the order-two case of Vélu's construction: the explicit Weierstrass model of the quotient of
an elliptic curve by the subgroup generated by a two-torsion point is again an elliptic curve.
Registering this ellipticity is what makes the explicit $2$-isogeny $W \to W/\{O,(x_0,y_0)\}$
available as a map of elliptic curves; it is used in the construction of modular polynomial data and
in the analysis of Tate points.

